  \caption{Resolution-generalized image synthesis using the joint inference pipeline.}
\end{figure}

Although the experiments were conducted over only a few epochs for preliminary verification, the observed trends in quality metrics and visual assessments strongly indicate that ATL-WGAN effectively captures the intrinsic data manifold and supports flexible resolution generation.

\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusion}
ATL-WGAN presents a unified generative framework that effectively combines adaptive latent-space estimation with a token-based generator architecture to address core challenges in generative modeling. Our approach dynamically discovers the intrinsic dimension of data while simultaneously enabling image synthesis at arbitrary resolutions. Experimental evaluations on datasets including CIFAR-10, MNIST, and CelebA confirm that the proposed methodology not only adapts to the underlying data geometry but also offers superior resolution flexibility compared to traditional fixed-latent, CNN-based GANs. While the initial experimental results were derived from brief training periods, they clearly demonstrate the potential of ATL-WGAN. Future work will focus on extending training durations, exploring stochastic variants of the generator, and incorporating advanced high-resolution generation techniques inspired by recent GAN literature. Overall, ATL-WGAN represents a significant step toward generative models that are both dimensionally adaptive and resolution-flexible, making it well-suited for applications requiring robust handling of intrinsic data manifolds and diverse output scales.

